The C# code provided has a comment that starts with "@@@" and states that if the return value of the `IndexOf` method is non-negative, then the value `x` is present in the old array list. This means that if the method returns a value larger than or equal to 0, that means that `x` is indeed in the list. So, this is what the appropriate `Debug.Assert` statement would be. 

Please note that `Debug.Assert` is used for debugging purposes. This code will only run if the DEBUG CONFIGURATION is set. If the condition is not met, it will not throw any error - it just silently fails. 

Remember to add the `System.Diagnostics` namespace to your C# code if you plan on using the `Debug.Assert` statement in it. 

```cs
using System.Diagnostics;
